The '''Anhangá curse''' ({{Langx|bl|Maldición de Anhangá}}; {{Langx|kp|Jo'á||binding}}) is a traditional indigenous and religious belief prevalent in [[Balisca]], linking major natural phenomena to the actions of protective nature spirits. The term "Anhangá" itself refers to the common name for the [[Baliscan archipelago]] amongst the [[Kurupiranga]], often translated as 'land of spirits'. The belief system is particularly associated with [[W:Native American religions|traditional indigenous cosmologies]] and finds resonance within the religious views of [[Candomblé]] and [[Lucumí Tarica]] adherents across Balisca.

== Etymology and Origin ==
The concept of the "Anhangá curse" draws directly from the [[Kurupiran language|Kurupirangüe]] term for Balisca, ''Anhangá'', which describes the archipelago as a realm teeming with spiritual presence—a "land of spirits". This linguistic root highlights the belief's indigenous origins, where the natural world is imbued with spiritual agency. The development of the "curse" as a concept is rooted in the traditional spiritual understanding that the land and its resources are guarded by powerful, sentient spirits, a view shared and reinforced by the cosmological perspectives of syncretic religions like Candomblé and Lucumí Tarica (which are representative of the [[W:Orisha|orixá]] and carried out by the [[W:Jinn|jinn]]).

== Nature and manifestations ==
The Anhangá curse posits that major natural disasters are not merely random occurrences but are spiritual manifestations of these nature spirits defending their domains. This core principle is articulated in the belief that the spirits "[...] does not kill to devour, but to restore and to avenge those victimized by insatiable hunters". This implies that environmental harm, exploitation, or disrespect towards the land and its resources can provoke the spirits, leading to retributive natural events.

The manifestations of the Anhangá curse are believed to occur across the Baliscan archipelago and include a range of severe natural phenomena:
* '''Earthquakes''': Particularly notable are the earthquakes caused by the [[Yawar Sypa fault-line]], which passes through the [[Castejón]] Metropolis. The destructive earthquakes in [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|1924]] and [[1982 Tava Morotí earthquake|1982]], which caused significant damage and high casualties in Castejón, are colloquially referred to as the "Alpujarran manifestation of the 'Anhangá curse'". Minor earthquakes are also seen as regular, lesser expressions.
